# `corners[(i + 1) % corners.length]`: the next element round the ring. A remainder by the length of the very list that is read is an index of it, so the look ahead of the loop takes the list among those
# that are tested in front, as it does for `corners[i]`. What hangs on that, step by step: the list is asked in front for its storage and whether it is as born; no read of the turn can run other code;
# so the variables of the file (frame, place) cannot change in the loop and are read in front of it; so the helper, which reads elements of what stands in front, is copied into the loop (frame 1).
# f1 = roundTheRing
